            <div xml:id="d481">

                <pb facs="0570" n="472" sameAs="frus:frus1932v04/pg472"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">393.11/1415: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China (Johnson) to the
                            Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peiping</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1932-02-07T10:00:00+08:00">February 7, 1932—10
                        a.m.</date><lb/>[Received February 7—2:25 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>205. Following from American Consul General at Hankow: <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“February 6, 4 p.m. Following telegram received from Bishop O’Shea at Kanchow, Kiangsi:
                            ‘Kanchow besieged by big Red army, please urge Nanchang send
                            succor’.</p>
                        <p>See Legation’s telegram of March 12, 11 a.m., 1930.<anchor n="9"
                                xml:id="d481-f1"/></p>
                        <p>I am wiring Nanchang Government requesting protection for O’Shea and Father
                                Lewis (not registered, legal name unknown) who
                            are the only two Americans in Kanchow.</p>
                        <p>Nanking informed.”</p>
                    </quote></p>
                <closer>For the Minister:<lb/><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Perkins</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
